WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

s1-1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.264
A jam to clear, a line to break, a vessel to open.

s1-2
00:00:05.264 --> 00:00:09.664
You know the job. Before your hands move, give it two minutes.

s1-3
00:00:09.664 --> 00:00:11.557
That's the 2-minute rule.

s2-1
00:00:12.647 --> 00:00:18.707
It's a short pause, on purpose, at the point of work: where you do the job.

s2-2
00:00:18.707 --> 00:00:26.184
Do it before you start, and again after any break, handover, interruption or change.

s2-3
00:00:26.184 --> 00:00:32.246
Two minutes is a guide, not an exact time. What matters is the pause.

s3-1
00:00:32.246 --> 00:00:38.018
You already do this for product: line clearance, right before each run.

s3-2
00:00:38.018 --> 00:00:41.682
The 2-minute rule is line clearance for people.

s3-3
00:00:41.682 --> 00:00:48.032
It adds to your procedures, permits and lockout. It never replaces them.

s3-4
00:00:48.032 --> 00:00:51.440
It catches what the plan missed, because you're there.

s4-1
00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.938
Stop: hands off.

s4-2
00:00:53.938 --> 00:00:58.348
Look: around the job, and compare it with the plan.

s4-3
00:00:58.348 --> 00:01:03.031
Ask four questions: What's different? What could hurt us?

s4-4
00:01:03.031 --> 00:01:06.290
Checked or assumed? What if it goes wrong?

s4-5
00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:12.855
Checked or assumed? It means: did you look or test it yourself, or are you guessing?

s4-6
00:01:12.855 --> 00:01:17.690
Decide: Go, fix it, or stop and get help.

s4-7
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.706
Fix it only if you're trained and authorized, and it's a step in your procedure.

s4-8
00:01:23.706 --> 00:01:30.939
No permit yet? Someone else's unfinished work? Not authorized? Stop and get help.

s4-9
00:01:30.939 --> 00:01:35.545
Not sure, or the fix doesn't work? Stop and get help.

s5-1
00:01:37.495 --> 00:01:42.030
First, a routine pause: you're about to clean a weighing booth.

s5-2
00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:49.011
What's different? Nothing. What could hurt us? Leftover powder and the cleaning chemical.

s5-3
00:01:49.011 --> 00:01:55.878
Gloves and goggles: on. Checked or assumed? Checked: the airflow light is green.

s5-4
00:01:55.878 --> 00:01:59.386
What if it goes wrong? The spill kit is right there.

s5-5
00:02:01.736 --> 00:02:04.101
Go. That took a few seconds.

s6-1
00:02:05.351 --> 00:02:07.883
Most pauses end like that.

s6-2
00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:13.734
Sometimes a question catches a problem. That's a save. Here are six.

s6-3
00:02:13.734 --> 00:02:19.221
They're examples built from common pharma hazards, not real events.

s6-4
00:02:19.221 --> 00:02:26.469
In each save, the video waits. Find the problem and pick your call, before we show it.

s7-1
00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:32.809
Save one: you take over a tablet press at handover.

s7-2
00:02:32.809 --> 00:02:34.683
What would make you stop here?

s7-3
00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:42.441
What's different? The note says the last shift started a tooling change.

s7-4
00:02:42.441 --> 00:02:47.786
Guard open. Tooling half in. No lock on the power switch.

s7-5
00:02:47.786 --> 00:02:48.956
Your call?

s7-6
00:02:51.106 --> 00:02:55.769
Stop and get help, because it's someone else's unfinished work.

s7-7
00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:59.887
Your lead comes, thanks you, and checks with the last shift.

s7-8
00:02:59.887 --> 00:03:07.073
An authorized press setter locks out, tries START, then finishes and counts the tooling.

s7-9
00:03:07.073 --> 00:03:14.533
Guard closed, area clear, setter's lock removed, interlock tested. Then Go.

s7-10
00:03:14.533 --> 00:03:17.958
Nobody starts that press with its tooling half in.

s8-1
00:03:20.348 --> 00:03:25.605
Save two: you add powder to a reactor that holds flammable solvent.

s8-2
00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:30.485
It's inerted: filled with nitrogen to keep the oxygen out.

s8-3
00:03:30.485 --> 00:03:32.359
What would make you stop here?

s8-4
00:03:35.109 --> 00:03:40.938
What could hurt us? A static spark at the open manway, with solvent vapor.

s8-5
00:03:40.938 --> 00:03:47.792
Checked or assumed? Oxygen is below the limit. But the grounding indicator is red.

s8-6
00:03:47.792 --> 00:03:48.962
Your call?

s8-7
00:03:51.112 --> 00:03:56.225
Fix it: your procedure lets you clip the grounding clamp onto the drum.

s8-8
00:03:56.225 --> 00:04:02.119
The indicator turns green, and the drum liner is static-safe. Then Go.

s8-9
00:04:02.119 --> 00:04:04.181
The spark never happens.

s9-1
00:04:06.571 --> 00:04:13.962
Save three: you're a contractor. At a product change, you'll open a dryer full of nitrogen.

s9-2
00:04:13.962 --> 00:04:15.835
What would make you stop here?

s9-3
00:04:18.585 --> 00:04:25.202
What could hurt us? Low-oxygen air inside. Nitrogen has no color or smell.

s9-4
00:04:25.202 --> 00:04:32.692
Checked or assumed? Is the nitrogen line closed with a solid plate, and the air inside tested?

s9-5
00:04:32.692 --> 00:04:35.474
No test, no permit yet.

s9-6
00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:36.644
Your call?

s9-7
00:04:38.794 --> 00:04:44.013
Stop and get help, because opening it needs a gas test and a permit.

s9-8
00:04:44.013 --> 00:04:53.331
An authorized team isolates, tests, ventilates and retests. The permit is issued. Then Go.

s9-9
00:04:53.331 --> 00:04:55.320
No permit, no Go.

s9-10
00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.920
Nobody leans into air with too little oxygen.

s10-1
00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:06.571
Save four: a blister packer jams where your jam tool can't reach.

s10-2
00:05:06.571 --> 00:05:09.422
Stopped isn't the same as isolated.

s10-3
00:05:09.422 --> 00:05:11.596
You lock out power and air.

s10-4
00:05:11.596 --> 00:05:13.470
What would make you stop here?

s10-5
00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.546
Checked or assumed? The gauge still shows trapped air.

s10-6
00:05:20.546 --> 00:05:21.716
Your call?

s10-7
00:05:23.866 --> 00:05:28.938
Fix it: your procedure lets you bleed the air, with your own lock on.

s10-8
00:05:28.938 --> 00:05:34.803
Bleed to zero and try START. Nothing moves. Then Go.

s10-9
00:05:34.803 --> 00:05:38.893
Your hand stays out of a machine that still had stored energy.

s11-1
00:05:41.283 --> 00:05:47.633
Save five: after a break, you weigh a potent active ingredient at an isolator.

s11-2
00:05:47.633 --> 00:05:49.507
What would make you stop here?

s11-3
00:05:52.257 --> 00:05:56.884
What's different? Nothing, and the pressure display is in range.

s11-4
00:05:56.884 --> 00:06:01.472
Checked or assumed? You check each glove. One has a cut.

s11-5
00:06:01.472 --> 00:06:02.641
Your call?

s11-6
00:06:04.791 --> 00:06:11.895
Stop and get help, because a cut glove is a break in the barrier. Your lead and QA must know.

s11-7
00:06:11.895 --> 00:06:17.485
An authorized person changes the glove and leak-tests it. Then Go.

s11-8
00:06:17.485 --> 00:06:23.548
That powder is strong, even in tiny amounts. It stays inside the isolator.

s12-1
00:06:25.938 --> 00:06:32.475
Save six: you're about to open a pipe at the transfer panel. That's a line break.

s12-2
00:06:32.475 --> 00:06:37.020
The CIP screen says the cleaning cycle is complete.

s12-3
00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:38.894
What would make you stop here?

s12-4
00:06:41.644 --> 00:06:46.485
What if it goes wrong? If the pipe still has pressure, where would it spray?

s12-5
00:06:46.485 --> 00:06:52.343
You lock out the supply valve and the pump, step to the side, and check the gauge.

s12-6
00:06:52.343 --> 00:06:55.762
It shows pressure, and the line is hot.

s12-7
00:06:55.762 --> 00:06:56.932
Your call?

s12-8
00:06:59.082 --> 00:07:07.424
Fix it. Your line-break procedure says: wait, vent, check for zero pressure and a cool line.

s12-9
00:07:08.374 --> 00:07:14.023
Then Go. The screen reports the cycle. The gauge reports your line.

s12-10
00:07:14.023 --> 00:07:17.373
No spray of hot cleaning chemical or steam.

s13-1
00:07:18.363 --> 00:07:24.014
Three fixes, three calls for help. All six end in Go, once it's safe.

s14-1
00:07:27.264 --> 00:07:35.420
Anyone can stop a job, staff or contractor, even on a late batch. No punishment, no blame.

s14-2
00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:42.171
Leaders come quickly and thank you. They fix the cause and tell you what changed.

s14-3
00:07:42.171 --> 00:07:46.831
The pause is not a form. Report what you caught the usual way.

s15-1
00:07:47.981 --> 00:07:56.085
Before you start, and after any break, handover, interruption or change: stop and look.

s15-2
00:07:56.085 --> 00:07:59.870
Ask: What's different? What could hurt us?

s15-3
00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:03.289
Checked or assumed? What if it goes wrong?

s15-4
00:08:03.289 --> 00:08:13.183
Decide: Go, fix it, or stop and get help. Fix it only if trained, authorized and in procedure.

s15-5
00:08:13.183 --> 00:08:16.241
Two minutes. Line clearance for people.

s15-6
00:08:18.591 --> 00:08:23.901
Find out who to call for help: days, nights and weekends.

s15-7
00:08:27.251 --> 00:08:32.704
Follow your site's procedures. This video is awareness training only.
